-- title:
--   Weighted deficits on leaves
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. Let leaves $s\in S$ carry weights $w_s$, and let a relation $R(v,s)$ say that a detector $v$ lies on the path of leaf $s$, with detector values $a_{v,s}$.
--
--   1. **Path deficit.**
--   $$
--   \sum_{v:\ R(v,s)}\bigl(1-a_{v,s}\bigr).
--   $$
--   2. **Bad leaves.** A leaf is bad if some incident detector has $a_{v,s}\le\frac12$.
--   3. **Bad leaf mass.** The total weight of bad leaves. If the weighted deficits are small, the bad mass is less than the total mass, so some leaf with positive weight is good: all its incident detectors exceed $\frac12$.
